\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Binary tetrahedral directions form the group algebra}
\date{2026-11-12}

\p{Let #{2T} be the group of \ref{fdip-00MK}. Let #{C} be the union
of #{\{\pm i,\pm j,\pm k\}} with the #{4} quaternions
##{\frac{-1+i+j+k}{2},\ 
\frac{-1+i-j-k}{2},\ 
\frac{-1-i+j-k}{2},\ 
\frac{-1-i-j+k}{2}.}
The set #{C} is conjugation-stable and generates #{2T}. The
direction algebra with the trivial cocycle is #{k[2T]}, of rank
#{24}. A Clifford algebra of a finite free module has rank a power
of #{2}, and #{24} lies outside that list. The set #{C} contains
an element of order #{3} and an element of order #{4}.}

\proof{
\p{The Lipschitz elements #{\pm i,\pm j,\pm k} square to #{-1}.
For #{\omega=(-1+i+j+k)/2}, the cross terms in
#{(i+j+k)^2} cancel, so
#{\omega^2=(-1-i-j-k)/2} and #{\omega^3=1}. Thus #{\omega} has
order #{3}, and it lies outside the Lipschitz units.}

\p{Conjugation by #{i} is the automorphism #{j\mapsto -j},
#{k\mapsto -k}. Expanding #{i\omega i^{-1}} with #{i^{-1}=-i}
gives #{(-1+i-j-k)/2}. The cyclic automorphism
#{i\mapsto j\mapsto k\mapsto i} fixes #{\omega} and sends
#{i\omega i^{-1}} to #{j\omega j^{-1}} and then to
#{k\omega k^{-1}}. Those two conjugates are the remaining displayed
quaternions. A conjugate of an element of order #{3} has order
#{3}, so all #{4} displayed quaternions lie outside the Lipschitz
units. The same automorphisms permute those #{4} elements. The
relations #{iji^{-1}=-j} and its cyclic copies permute
#{\{\pm i,\pm j,\pm k\}}.}

\p{The isomorphism of \ref{fdip-00MK} sends #{i}, #{j} and #{\omega}
to the matrices #{\iota}, #{\eta} and #{\rho}, and it sends
#{\kappa=\iota\eta} to the class of #{k}. The recorded cycle
#{\rho\iota\rho^{-1}=\kappa}, #{\rho\kappa\rho^{-1}=\eta} and
#{\rho\eta\rho^{-1}=\iota} therefore says
#{\omega i\omega^{-1}=k}, #{\omega k\omega^{-1}=j} and
#{\omega j\omega^{-1}=i}. The #{6} Lipschitz labels are stable under
conjugation by #{\omega}. For a conjugate
#{g=i\omega i^{-1}}, 
#{\omega g\omega^{-1}=(\omega i\omega^{-1})\,\omega\,(\omega i\omega^{-1})^{-1}},
which is a conjugate of #{\omega} by #{k} and hence one of the #{4}.
The same holds for the conjugates by #{j} and by #{k}. Since
#{i}, #{j} and #{\omega} generate a subgroup, and that subgroup
contains the order-#{8} group #{\{\pm 1,\pm i,\pm j,\pm k\}} and
the outside element #{\omega} of order #{3}, its order is a multiple
of #{24}. It equals #{2T}. Every conjugation is therefore a word in
conjugations that preserve #{C}, and #{C} generates #{2T}.}

\p{The trivial cocycle gives the group algebra, free of rank
#{24}. By \ref{fdip-00QB} a Clifford algebra of a free module of
finite rank #{r} is free of rank #{2^r}. The integer #{24} lies
outside that list of ranks.}
}
